From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] esp32: build TinyUSB 0.21 from the component registry.

The esp32 port builds TinyUSB from the espressif/tinyusb managed
component, pinned to micropython/tinyusb-espressif's
cherrypick/dwc2_zlp_fix branch: TinyUSB 0.18 plus the DWC2 OUT-endpoint
ZLP fix (hathach/tinyusb#3154, fixed by #3293). The pin's own comment
says it can go once that fix is released in espressif/tinyusb. It has
been: espressif/tinyusb 0.21.0~2 carries it (dcd_dwc2.c, "short packet
(including ZLP when byte_count == 0)"). Every other port already builds
0.21, from lib/tinyusb, so shared/tinyusb is written for it.

What this changes for a USB audio device: from 0.19 the audio class
finishes an isochronous OUT packet in the USB interrupt
(audiod_xfer_isr): it copies the packet into the endpoint FIFO, re-arms
the endpoint and calls tud_audio_rx_done_isr(). In 0.18 all of that
waited for tud_task(), which on this port runs in the MicroPython task
through the scheduler, so a busy interpreter left the endpoint unarmed
and lost packets. Measured on an ESP32-P4 sound card (usbif) with the
interpreter allocating in a tight loop: 91-95 % of packets delivered
on 0.18, heard as a tick every few tens of milliseconds.

The API changes that reach a user C module are 0.19's and 0.20's:
tud_audio_rx_done_post_read_cb() became tud_audio_rx_done_isr() and
runs in the ISR, and TUD_AUDIO_EP_SIZE() takes the bus speed as its
first argument. Nothing in MicroPython itself uses either.

One behaviour change to know about: 0.21's DWC2 driver arms an
isochronous OUT endpoint for the microframe after the current one,
whatever its interval (0.18 did that only for bInterval 1). A
high-speed isochronous OUT endpoint with a longer interval then waits
on the wrong frame parity and receives nothing; usbif's sound card at
bInterval 4 got no packets at all until it went back to 1, which the
interrupt-time re-arm makes safe.

The lockfiles under ports/esp32/lockfiles are not part of this patch.
The component manager re-solves on the first build after the manifest
changes and rewrites the target's lockfile, as it already does for any
overlay that edits idf_component.yml.

---
diff --git a/ports/esp32/main/idf_component.yml b/ports/esp32/main/idf_component.yml
index ddd7cb2..344f898 100644
--- a/ports/esp32/main/idf_component.yml
+++ b/ports/esp32/main/idf_component.yml
@@ -5,10 +5,10 @@ dependencies:
   espressif/tinyusb:
     rules:
       - if: "target in [esp32s2, esp32s3, esp32p4]"
-    # Temporary workaround for https://github.com/hathach/tinyusb/issues/3154
-    # Can be removed once fix is released in espressif/tinyusb
-    git: https://github.com/micropython/tinyusb-espressif.git
-    version: cherrypick/dwc2_zlp_fix
+    # 0.21 carries the OUT-endpoint ZLP fix (hathach/tinyusb#3293) that the
+    # cherrypick/dwc2_zlp_fix branch was holding, and it re-arms the audio
+    # class's isochronous OUT endpoint in the ISR, not in tud_task().
+    version: "0.21.0~2"
   espressif/esp_hosted:
     rules:
       - if: "target == esp32p4"
